  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 332 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nUpper level troughing along the eastern seaboard will move off into \nthe Atlantic over the weekend. Meanwhile, a shortwave perturbation is \ncurrently riding the monsoon high over the northern Great Plains. \nThis feature will dig south and deepen over the midwest, with some \nsolutions cutting off an upper low circulation just north of the \nOhio River valley. This is where the forecast starts to become a \nlittle bit more uncertain, especially regarding the evolution of \nthis system to start the next week. Ensemble cluster analysis is in \nagreement that anomalously deep troughing will overspread much of \nthe eastern CONUS starting on Sunday, which should increase chances \nof precipitation at least for the Deep South. At the surface, this \ntrough will induce a cold front that will push south into northern \nFlorida through the beginning of the week, providing additional \nforcing and ascent for storms. This front is unlikely to make its \nway into South Florida, keeping the higher moisture and chances of \nprecipitation over central Florida. There is also a signal for \nincreased concentrations of Saharan dust to make its way into the \nregion from the southeast. This would likely set up another \nsituation where there is a north to south moisture gradient along \nthe peninsula, with higher chances of precipitation closer to Lake \nOkeechobee and drier air hindering convection to the south. \n\nEnsemble clusters are currently split into two general solutions \nregarding the possible midwest upper low: a more progressive \nnorthern stream system, and a slower southern stream system. About \n60% of ensemble members show the upper low tracking northeast into \nthe Great Lakes through the middle of the week, but keeping \npositively tilted troughing over the South. About 40% of members \nshow the upper low tracking further into the mid-South and slower \nmoving. This solution would likely provide better forcing and \nmoisture for storms in South Florida. At the surface, winds are \nexpected to remain southwesterly as high pressure focuses over the \nsouthern Gulf. However, winds are expected to trend more southerly \nas the main trough axis swings through the region. After that, winds \nmay shift east for the middle of the week as the Atlantic ridge \nexpands westward.\n\nTo sum it up, it's a pretty low confidence forecast for the long \nterm due to the spread in solutions for several features upstream. \nIn general, PoPs will increase through the weekend and into next \nweek as better moisture works into the region. The unsettled weather \npattern will have better support for stronger thunderstorms with \nheavy rainfall and strong wind gusts being the main threats. The \ninteraction of periphery mid-level SAL could possibly contribute to \nsome severe wind gusts. Temperatures will remain warm through the \nperiod, with widespread moderate HeatRisk and isolated Major \nHeatRisk across most of South Florida.",
